# Original PAC14 timeout source assessment

Actual cause remains UNKNOWN. This read-only assessment does not change the fourteen controls or their outer60-second limit. The retained verbose/faulthandler diagnostic completes all14 original cases, exit0 in2.32879889398464 seconds, with all three source images unchanged. No30-second traceback was needed. The separately retained original-cjs.raw.log also contains14 completed cases and the source-contract success print; this note does not replace its parent-owned outer terminal receipt or the earlier whole414 timeout failure.

A concrete local liveness gap exists at tools/test/native_pac_source_fixture_test.py:116: process.stdout.readline() is unbounded and occurs before communicate(timeout=10). The nested child constructs SourceFixture before printing/flushing its first profile. native_pac_source_fixture.py:47–55 gives each of three OpenSSL subprocesses its own30-second timeout; no aggregate startup deadline limits that readiness read. Multiple fixtures also create new keys during this cohort. Thus delayed key generation/process scheduling can use the outer60 seconds without entering the test's communicate timeout. This is an executable source-supported potential wait, not a demonstrated cause of the observed sporadic timeout or proof of CPU contention. No failed-period child stack or per-method timing was retained by the original nonverbose CJS invocation.

The controlled threading seams were inspected separately. HeldBootstrap, HeldTail and HeldRequest have unbounded release.wait() calls (test lines145/267/324), but each owning test unconditionally release.set() in its finally path before final joins. Their assertion waits/joins use2 seconds; no missing release path was established. Direct server.shutdown() calls in controlled cleanup (lines192/379) are blocking BaseServer acknowledgements, so a dead or never-entered serving target could in general strand cleanup; the current supplied controls wait for actual target/start and release it, and no reachable race proving that stranded state was found. No broad global wait substitution was found: the interrupted start mocks a retained worker's own _started.wait.

Production fixture close uses retained shutdown threads plus one3-second deadline for shutdown/serving/request joins. Failure preserves non-daemon ownership/debt instead of reporting closure. This can legitimately keep an interpreter alive under unresolved debt; this source scan does not prove that occurred in the failed run. HTTP socket and TLS handshake operations have explicit2/3-second timeouts. Numeric listener server_bind directly calls TCPServer.server_bind and does not call getfqdn; the original dedicated test verifies that. The localhost hostname-verification case intentionally still performs ordinary host resolution, but no actual DNS failure was observed.

The exact CJS receiver at test-native-pac-source-contract.cjs:704 keeps spawnSync timeout60000 and demands no error/signal, status0, actual14/OK and no skipped cases. No deadline increase, assertion weakening, source edit, fixture mock change, new replay, suite, native process or API was performed here. Parent's existing exact CJS receiving remains the only in-flight receiver. A future failed-period faulthandler/method trace could distinguish the readiness read from controlled cleanup; the present evidence cannot justify assigning the root cause.
